WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.122
This slide shows the main coding strategy behind the final exercises.

00:00:04.122 --> 00:00:11.557
The target is one row per patient with three measures: admission count, lab count, and prescription count.

00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:14.982
Patients with no activity must still remain.

00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:20.207
The safest solution is to aggregate each child table by patient_id first.

00:00:20.207 --> 00:00:25.222
After aggregation, each summary contains at most one row per patient.

00:00:25.222 --> 00:00:28.357
Then we LEFT JOIN those summaries to patients.

00:00:28.357 --> 00:00:33.857
Because the patient table is preserved, patients with no activity stay in the result.

00:00:33.857 --> 00:00:37.077
COALESCE turns missing counts into zero.

00:00:37.077 --> 00:00:40.572
This approach is very common in production analytics.

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:46.402
Imagine a customer dashboard with order count, support-ticket count, and refund count.

00:00:46.402 --> 00:00:52.172
Instead of joining raw order rows, raw ticket rows, and raw refund rows together,

00:00:52.172 --> 00:00:56.517
summarize each activity first. Then combine the summaries.

00:00:56.517 --> 00:01:03.357
Run the query and validate at least two patients: one with multiple activities and one with no activity.

00:01:03.357 --> 00:01:09.680
If the final result is one row per patient and the counts match the source data, the grain is protected.
